\begin{abstract}
We study a finite Boolean wagering problem with asymmetric consequences. A statement has a truth value in each possible world; a correct bet earns $a$ units and an incorrect bet loses $b$ units. The player may bet true with probability $r\in[0,1]$, bet false otherwise, and may optionally abstain for payoff zero. For arbitrary finite rational world weights, we derive the exact expected payoff from the total true-world and false-world masses. Every randomized value is an affine combination of the two pure values. Hence randomization cannot outperform the better pure action, and a positive randomized edge exists exactly when a pure action has positive value. Under a normalized prior and $a+b>0$, positive profit is possible exactly when the true probability $\pi$ satisfies $\pi<a/(a+b)$ or $\pi>b/(a+b)$. Adding abstention produces value $\max\{0,V_F,V_T\}$ and dominates every randomized Boolean wager.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Binary decisions are rarely symmetric. The harms of a false medical diagnosis, an undetected fraud, and a mistaken forecast need not equal the benefits of correct decisions. Once rewards and penalties are attached to truth and falsehood, probability must be interpreted through a payoff table.

We consider a finite world space, a Boolean statement evaluated in each world, and a rational prior. Correctness earns $a$ and error pays $-b$. Three reductions solve the model. World-level detail compresses to the total true and false masses; randomized payoff is affine in the mixing probability; and a zero-payoff pass option reduces optimization to a maximum of three explicit values. These facts yield exact profitability thresholds and a minimal model of selective prediction.

\section{Finite-world model}
Let $W$ be a finite set, let $s:W\to\{\mathrm{false},\mathrm{true}\}$, and let $\mu:W\to\mathbb{Q}$ assign rational weight to each world.

\begin{definition}[Truth and falsehood masses]
Define
\[
T=\sum_{\omega:s(\omega)=\mathrm{true}}\mu(\omega),\qquad
F=\sum_{\omega:s(\omega)=\mathrm{false}}\mu(\omega).
\]
A prior is normalized when $\sum_{\omega\in W}\mu(\omega)=1$.
\end{definition}

For a probabilistic interpretation the weights are also nonnegative, although the algebraic identities below do not require positivity.

\begin{lemma}[Mass partition]
For every finite weighted world model,
\[
T+F=\sum_{\omega\in W}\mu(\omega).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Every world belongs to exactly one of the disjoint classes determined by $s$, so its weight occurs exactly once on the left. Summing the pointwise partition proves the identity.
\end{proof}

Under normalization, write $\pi=T$; then $F=1-\pi$.

\begin{definition}[Pure asymmetric payoff]
For rational parameters $a,b$, the payoff of a Boolean bet $q$ in world $\omega$ is
\[
P(q,\omega)=\begin{cases}a,&q=s(\omega),\\-b,&q\ne s(\omega).\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Randomized strategy]
For $r\in[0,1]$, bet true with probability $r$ and false with probability $1-r$. Define
\[
P_r(\omega)=rP(\mathrm{true},\omega)+(1-r)P(\mathrm{false},\omega),
\qquad
V(r)=\sum_{\omega\in W}\mu(\omega)P_r(\omega).
\]
Write $V_F=V(0)$ and $V_T=V(1)$.
\end{definition}

\section{Exact payoff formulas}
\begin{lemma}[Per-world randomized payoff]
For every world $\omega$,
\[
P_r(\omega)=\begin{cases}
(a+b)r-b,&s(\omega)=\mathrm{true},\\
a-(a+b)r,&s(\omega)=\mathrm{false}.
\end{cases}
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
In a true world the mixture earns $ra-(1-r)b$; in a false world it earns $-rb+(1-r)a$. Expanding gives the stated expressions.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Closed-form expectation]
For arbitrary finite rational weights,
\[
V(r)=\bigl((a+b)r-b\bigr)T+\bigl(a-(a+b)r\bigr)F.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Split the expectation into true and false worlds. The preceding lemma makes the payoff constant within each class; factoring those constants leaves $T$ and $F$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Pure values]
\[
V_F=aF-bT,\qquad V_T=aT-bF.
\]
If the prior is normalized, then
\[
V_F=a-(a+b)\pi,\qquad V_T=(a+b)\pi-b.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Substitute $r=0$ and $r=1$ in the closed form. Under normalization substitute $F=1-\pi$.
\end{proof}

\section{Affine randomization and optimality}
\begin{theorem}[Affine Randomization Theorem]
For every rational $r$,
\[
V(r)=rV_T+(1-r)V_F.
\]
Thus for $r\in[0,1]$ the value lies on the line segment joining the two pure values.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand $r(aT-bF)+(1-r)(aF-bT)$ and collect the coefficients of $T$ and $F$. The result is the closed-form expectation above.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Pure-Strategy Optimality]
For $0\le r\le1$,
\[
V(r)\le\max\{V_F,V_T\},
\qquad
\max_{0\le r\le1}V(r)=\max\{V_F,V_T\}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $M=\max\{V_F,V_T\}$. Since $r$ and $1-r$ are nonnegative, the affine identity gives $V(r)\le rM+(1-r)M=M$. An endpoint attaining $M$ gives equality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Positive-edge equivalence]
\[
\bigl(\exists r\in[0,1]:V(r)>0\bigr)
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
V_F>0\ \text{or}\ V_T>0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A positive endpoint itself supplies a strategy. Conversely, a positive mixed value is at most the larger endpoint, so that endpoint must be positive.
\end{proof}

This endpoint result applies to a fixed prior. Mixed strategies can remain essential in interactive games where an opponent responds to predictable action; no such response occurs here.

\section{Sharp profitability thresholds}
Assume henceforth that the prior is normalized and $a+b>0$.

\begin{theorem}[Sharp Profitability Thresholds]
There is an $r\in[0,1]$ with $V(r)>0$ if and only if
\[
\pi<\frac{a}{a+b}\qquad\text{or}\qquad \frac{b}{a+b}<\pi.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Positive-edge equivalence reduces the claim to $V_F>0$ or $V_T>0$. The normalized formulas give
\[
V_F>0\iff a-(a+b)\pi>0\iff\pi<\frac{a}{a+b},
\]
and
\[
V_T>0\iff(a+b)\pi-b>0\iff\frac{b}{a+b}<\pi,
\]
where the positive denominator preserves both inequality directions.
\end{proof}

When $a,b\ge0$ and $b\ge a$, the interval $[a/(a+b),b/(a+b)]$ is a no-positive-edge region. When $a>b$, the thresholds overlap in reverse order, and both bets can be profitable in the overlap; the theorem's disjunction covers both regimes.

\begin{example}[Symmetric stakes]
For $a=b=1$, $V_F=1-2\pi$ and $V_T=2\pi-1$. Positive value exists precisely when $\pi\ne1/2$; at $\pi=1/2$, every mixture has value zero.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Punitive errors]
For $a=1,b=2$, $V_F=1-3\pi$ and $V_T=3\pi-2$. Bet false below $1/3$, bet true above $2/3$, and neither bet has positive value on $[1/3,2/3]$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Favorable rewards]
For $a=2,b=1$, $V_F=2-3\pi$ and $V_T=3\pi-1$. Both bets are positive for $1/3<\pi<2/3$, reflecting favorable offered odds.
\end{example}

\section{Abstention}
\begin{definition}[Abstention value]
When passing yields zero, define
\[
A=\max\{0,V_F,V_T\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Nonnegativity and optimal passing]
One always has $A\ge0$. If $V_F\le0$ and $V_T\le0$, then $A=0$, so abstention is optimal.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Zero is one candidate in the maximum. If both other candidates are nonpositive, the maximum equals zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dominance over randomized betting]
For every $r\in[0,1]$,
\[
V(r)\le A.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Pure-strategy optimality gives $V(r)\le\max\{V_F,V_T\}$, and this maximum is at most $A$.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms and complexity}
For an explicit table of $n=|W|$ worlds, aggregate $T$ and $F$ in one pass. This costs $O(n)$ time and $O(1)$ auxiliary space. Thereafter every strategy value is computed in $O(1)$ time from the closed form.

The optimal action with abstention is obtained by computing $V_F=aF-bT$ and $V_T=aT-bF$, then comparing $0,V_F,V_T$. Return false when $V_F$ is a positive maximum, true when $V_T$ is a positive maximum, and pass when both are nonpositive. Ties may be resolved among maximizing actions.

For a visual strategy curve, evaluate $V(r_k)=r_kV_T+(1-r_k)V_F$ at $r_k=k/m$. The $m+1$ points cost $O(m)$ time and lie exactly on a line; the sampling illustrates rather than estimates the function.

\section{Applications}
In cost-sensitive classification, $\pi$ is a posterior class probability and the theorem supplies utility-adjusted classification thresholds. In selective prediction, uncertain cases may be routed to a reviewer; abstention is optimal precisely when neither label has positive value. In event contracts, transaction terms shift break-even probability away from one half. In logical uncertainty, the true and false masses summarize how belief divides across possible completions; an independent random choice cannot manufacture information absent from that prior-payoff relation.

\section{Scope and future work}
The model is finite, static, and risk-neutral. Rational weights provide exact arithmetic; finite real weights obey the same identities. A probability interpretation requires nonnegative normalized weights. The threshold theorem needs $a+b>0$ so division preserves inequalities. Abstention is assigned zero cost, whereas practical deferral may incur delay or review cost.

The affine conclusion can fail to settle richer models involving nonlinear utility, adversarial response, information acquisition, or constraints coupling multiple wagers. Natural extensions include measure-theoretic world spaces, sequential Bayesian updating and regret, explicit transaction fees and bid--ask spreads, a two-player minimax formulation, and proper scoring rules for reported probabilities.

\section{Conclusion}
All world-level detail in the asymmetric Boolean wager compresses to $T$ and $F$. Every randomized strategy is an affine combination of the pure endpoints, so randomization cannot improve on the better pure bet. Under normalization and $a+b>0$, positive profit exists exactly outside the two threshold inequalities
\[
\pi<\frac{a}{a+b}\qquad\text{or}\qquad\pi>\frac{b}{a+b}.
\]
Adding abstention raises the value to $\max\{0,V_F,V_T\}$ and makes inaction optimal whenever both bets are nonpositive. The resulting procedure is exact and simple: aggregate belief, evaluate the endpoints, and act only when an endpoint clears zero.

